  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough Monday...\n\nBehind the showers over the east this morning, pressure rises \nworking into the region behind the Pacific trough were bringing \ngusty westerly winds to the area. Strongest winds have been over \nthe western foothills where gusts to 50 mph have been observed. \nExpect the winds to continue through the afternoon, backing off \nthis evening. Another wave currently in Oregon will slide east \nthrough Idaho and Wyoming this evening and overnight, keeping \nmountain snow showers in the forecast for south and west aspects \nof the Beartooth/Absarokas and southern Bighorns. Accumulations \nwill be light overall, but a few inches are possible in the \nmountains outside Cooke City. A few snow showers may clip far \nsoutheast MT late tonight into early tomorrow morning, but the \nmajority of the energy will remain to the south.\n\nWednesday into the first part of the weekend, the pattern looks \nto transition to southwest flow aloft over the region as troughing\ndevelops along the West Coast and ridging noses in. Dry \nconditions will prevail into Friday, before energy advecting in \nfrom the southwest brings a return of snow showers to the western \nmountains late Friday into Saturday. Breezy conditions can be \nexpected at times, but strong winds are not expected. High \ntemperatures are forecast from the 40s to 50s most days, warmest\non Wednesday and Saturday. While still aways out, there is \npotential for more unsettled conditions late Sunday into early \nnext week as ensembles and clusters prog a large trough and \nassociated low to push inland into the Rockies. This could bring\ncooler conditions and precipitation to portions of the region. STP",
